WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.460
So, you've probably had this exact experience, right?

00:02.580 --> 00:08.140
You're sitting there, um, watching some tech CEO standing on a stage, usually with dramatic

00:08.140 --> 00:12.540
lighting, and they are promising this completely world-altering technology.

00:12.760 --> 00:14.860
Yeah, the classic visionary pitch.

00:14.980 --> 00:15.180
Right.

00:15.640 --> 00:16.060
Exactly.

00:16.660 --> 00:19.620
And you're watching this and wondering, like, is this real?

00:20.220 --> 00:23.820
Or am I basically just being sold a sci-fi novel right now?

00:24.260 --> 00:27.800
It hits a question everyone is asking lately, especially in this current climate.

00:27.800 --> 00:32.600
Well, today, we're actually going to equip you with an analytical toolkit to answer that

00:32.600 --> 00:33.220
exact question.

00:33.980 --> 00:38.620
The mission for today's Deep Dive is to figure out how to separate actual physical technological

00:38.620 --> 00:41.660
progress from, you know, heavily funded hype.

00:41.940 --> 00:46.220
We're going to dismantle what you might call the faith-based logic of Silicon Valley.

00:46.500 --> 00:46.820
Yes.

00:47.180 --> 00:52.220
And we're doing this by looking at this incredibly fascinating independent research paper that

00:52.220 --> 00:54.420
came out in October of 2026.

00:54.640 --> 00:57.040
It's by a researcher who goes by the name Flexium.

00:57.040 --> 00:59.400
And it's called Unauthorized Futures.

00:59.800 --> 01:01.260
Let me make sure I get the subtitle right.

01:01.880 --> 01:05.840
Possibility, Demonstration, and the Limits of Retroactive Vindication.

01:06.560 --> 01:09.960
It's quite a title, but it's an incredibly vital piece of writing.

01:10.240 --> 01:10.720
It really is.

01:11.020 --> 01:15.940
And to kind of set the scene for why this paper matters right now, Flexium points to some recent

01:15.940 --> 01:18.120
commentary from Charlie Worzel over at The Atlantic.

01:18.640 --> 01:20.320
He writes the Galaxy Brain column.

01:20.500 --> 01:20.680
Right.

01:20.820 --> 01:23.360
Worzel does a great job of capturing the cultural zeitgeist.

01:23.360 --> 01:24.000
He does.

01:24.640 --> 01:28.320
And Worzel basically connected three massive cultural moments that all happened around

01:28.320 --> 01:28.920
the same time.

01:29.560 --> 01:33.760
First, you had this literal flying Tesla prototype being demonstrated.

01:33.960 --> 01:36.000
Which was visually spectacular, honestly.

01:36.200 --> 01:37.160
Oh, absolutely wild.

01:37.540 --> 01:42.140
Then second, you had this new documentary drop called You Can See Everything, which is

01:42.140 --> 01:47.580
all about Elizabeth Holmes' partner, Billy Evans, trying to assemble like a version two of

01:47.580 --> 01:47.980
Theranos.

01:48.400 --> 01:50.760
A fascinating choice, given the history there.

01:50.900 --> 01:51.580
Yeah, to say the least.

01:51.580 --> 01:56.680
And then third, you have the current artificial intelligence boom, which is just driven by

01:56.680 --> 02:00.080
these staggering, almost hallucinatory financial promises.

02:00.780 --> 02:05.420
And this is where the core thesis of Flexium's paper really shines, because we live in this

02:05.420 --> 02:11.660
culture where claims about things that fundamentally don't exist yet are treated as completely solid

02:11.660 --> 02:14.180
ground for making massive commitments today.

02:14.400 --> 02:15.760
Okay, let's unpack this.

02:15.760 --> 02:21.580
Because on the surface, those three things, cars, blood tests, and bots, seem completely

02:21.580 --> 02:22.060
unrelated.

02:22.600 --> 02:25.880
They do seem different, but they all share this unified ethos.

02:26.240 --> 02:28.040
It's the cult of the builder, right?

02:28.480 --> 02:33.640
This idea of a high agency visionary who is perceived by the market as never actually being

02:33.640 --> 02:35.500
wrong, just, you know, being early.

02:35.620 --> 02:36.380
Being early, right.

02:36.380 --> 02:37.600
That classic excuse.

02:37.860 --> 02:38.360
Exactly.

02:38.360 --> 02:43.520
The paper points out how persuasion has completely overtaken actual product development in these

02:43.520 --> 02:43.960
spaces.

02:44.540 --> 02:46.000
Wait, let me push back on that for a second, though.

02:46.520 --> 02:47.980
Aren't these totally different scenarios?

02:48.760 --> 02:55.340
Like, Tesla actually showed a physical car defying gravity on a stage, whereas Theranos literally

02:55.340 --> 02:57.580
faked blood test results in the back room.

02:58.240 --> 02:59.280
That's a really fair point.

02:59.280 --> 03:04.140
It just feels like we're comparing a magician pulling off a crazy, highly coordinated stunt

03:04.140 --> 03:07.840
to someone who's just straight up lying about even owning a rabbit, you know?

03:08.020 --> 03:08.780
I love that analogy.

03:09.220 --> 03:12.480
And you're right, the paper isn't equating the level of fraud here.

03:12.580 --> 03:15.840
Obviously, one is an engineering stunt and the other is criminal fraud.

03:16.360 --> 03:19.840
What Flyxion is doing is equating the epistemic pattern.

03:20.220 --> 03:23.280
Epistemic meaning, like, how we know what we know?

03:23.640 --> 03:24.120
Precisely.

03:24.120 --> 03:29.380
In all three of these examples, a proposition about a future state is being used as a premise

03:29.380 --> 03:31.260
for decisions that are being made right now.

03:31.620 --> 03:34.160
By people who are going to be the ones holding the bag if it fails.

03:34.620 --> 03:35.140
Exactly.

03:36.020 --> 03:41.240
Decisions made today based on tomorrow's promises by people bearing the actual cost.

03:41.860 --> 03:46.140
And that realization that we are constantly confusing different types of future promises

03:46.140 --> 03:49.320
leads directly into Flyxion's four-part framework.

03:49.700 --> 03:51.380
This framework is brilliant.

03:51.380 --> 03:57.920
So the paper defines four predicates, basically four logical hurdles that any technological claim

03:57.920 --> 03:59.280
about the future has to clear.

04:00.040 --> 04:01.280
And they build on each other.

04:01.600 --> 04:04.160
So the very first one is possibility.

04:04.540 --> 04:09.800
This just means, like, do the laws of physics or biology actually permit this thing to exist?

04:10.300 --> 04:12.080
It's the lowest possible bar.

04:12.520 --> 04:12.800
Right.

04:13.060 --> 04:15.240
It's just saying it isn't impossible.

04:15.240 --> 04:17.980
Then the second one is reachability.

04:18.580 --> 04:23.260
Meaning, okay, it's possible, but is there actually a sequence of achievable steps to get there?

04:23.660 --> 04:27.400
Steps that respect the real-world constraints of time, money, and materials.

04:27.740 --> 04:27.860
Yeah.

04:28.480 --> 04:30.220
And then third is sustainability.

04:30.940 --> 04:35.240
Can you maintain and operate this thing continuously, or is it just a one-off trick?

04:35.700 --> 04:37.800
This is where so many projects fail.

04:37.800 --> 04:40.060
And finally, the fourth one is authorization.

04:40.640 --> 04:46.540
Does the current evidence actually justify the specific action or the investment you are asking for meat today?

04:47.280 --> 04:51.740
So let's unpack the logical gaps between these, because that's where the manipulation happens.

04:51.880 --> 04:52.160
Right.

04:52.740 --> 04:53.560
Think about Theranos.

04:54.440 --> 04:58.480
A founder standing up and saying a pinprick of blood can yield hundreds of test results,

04:58.720 --> 05:00.280
that is just defining an endpoint.

05:00.420 --> 05:01.440
That's possibility.

05:01.960 --> 05:05.420
Just because the biomarkers exist in the blood doesn't mean you can extract them.

05:05.420 --> 05:06.060
Exactly.

05:06.540 --> 05:12.080
It does not mean a biochemical or manufacturing path actually exists to build the machine.

05:12.200 --> 05:13.240
They had no reachability.

05:14.000 --> 05:16.220
Now, compare that to the Tesla Roadster stunt.

05:16.680 --> 05:16.940
Right.

05:17.020 --> 05:19.420
With the Tesla, they used compressed air thrusters,

05:19.640 --> 05:25.240
like 600 liters of compressed air blasted across 10 thrusters just to get it to do that flip.

05:25.560 --> 05:30.360
So that stunt proved reachability, but only a tiny fragment of it, right?

05:30.540 --> 05:30.760
Right.

05:30.760 --> 05:35.160
It proved a prototype could do it exactly once under highly controlled conditions.

05:35.420 --> 05:37.940
It proved absolutely zero about sustainability.

05:38.680 --> 05:43.380
Because you can't drive to the grocery store with 600 liters of compressed air strapped to your roof.

05:43.520 --> 05:43.880
Exactly.

05:44.160 --> 05:50.940
It proves nothing about road legality, safety, or mass manufacturability for a 2028 release.

05:51.360 --> 05:53.300
Let me see if I can ground this with an analogy.

05:54.340 --> 05:58.320
So possibility is me saying, theoretically, humans can live on Mars.

05:58.740 --> 05:59.320
It's a rock.

05:59.620 --> 06:00.320
It has some water.

06:00.320 --> 06:02.180
It doesn't violate physics.

06:03.660 --> 06:08.400
Reachability is me saying, we have the rocket designs and the fuel to get a human body there.

06:08.920 --> 06:09.260
Yes.

06:09.980 --> 06:17.420
Sustainability is saying, we can actually grow potatoes in the soil and keep the oxygen scrubbers running for 20 years without everyone dying.

06:17.880 --> 06:19.820
Which is an entirely different level of engineering.

06:19.960 --> 06:20.120
Right.

06:20.120 --> 06:28.720
But then authorization is me knocking on your door today and asking you to empty your entire 401k to buy a timeshare in a Martian crater.

06:29.080 --> 06:29.960
I love that.

06:30.140 --> 06:34.080
Just because Mars exists doesn't mean I have the right to take your life savings today.

06:34.620 --> 06:39.460
What's fascinating here is how promoters intentionally exploit this exact gap.

06:39.460 --> 06:48.620
They take the weakest claim, possibility, which is usually backed by immense founder charisma, and they use it to underwrite the strongest claim, which is authorization.

06:48.620 --> 06:54.380
They're asking you to commit real resources right now based on a theoretical endpoint.

06:54.800 --> 06:55.120
Exactly.

06:55.580 --> 07:04.620
And once we understand that promoters are improperly jumping straight to authorization, we have to look at how they manage to bypass the normal checks and balances.

07:05.220 --> 07:05.500
Right.

07:05.560 --> 07:09.940
Because normally institutions don't just hand over a billion dollars because someone gave a good TED talk.

07:10.560 --> 07:11.760
Well, they shouldn't anyway.

07:11.860 --> 07:12.320
They shouldn't.

07:13.360 --> 07:17.160
Flyxion breaks down the theoretical pipeline that a claim is supposed to pass through.

07:17.320 --> 07:18.260
There are five stages.

07:18.260 --> 07:18.980
Let's run through.

07:19.420 --> 07:20.660
First is generation.

07:20.940 --> 07:21.840
You just have the idea.

07:22.360 --> 07:27.260
Second is inscription, which means writing the idea down in a way that can actually be inspected.

07:27.480 --> 07:29.100
Hard schematics, code, assays.

07:29.300 --> 07:30.140
Not just a pitch deck.

07:30.500 --> 07:30.740
No.

07:31.500 --> 07:32.100
Real data.

07:32.620 --> 07:37.280
Third is evaluation, where that data is rigorously tested against independent evidence.

07:37.460 --> 07:37.660
Okay.

07:37.800 --> 07:39.980
Generation, inscription, evaluation.

07:40.400 --> 07:45.020
Then fourth is authorization, where it's formally approved for a specific action.

07:45.020 --> 07:50.380
And fifth is the commitment, the irreversible action of spending the money or deploying the tech.

07:50.740 --> 07:52.620
So how are the visionaries hacking this pipeline?

07:52.940 --> 07:55.960
By compressing or bypassing the middle stages completely.

07:56.680 --> 07:59.200
Theranos basically bypassed inscription entirely.

07:59.200 --> 08:06.000
They hid behind trade secrets to avoid ever putting anything inspectable on paper, which meant they could avoid evaluation.

08:06.800 --> 08:10.720
Oh, because if there's no data to evaluate, no one can prove you wrong.

08:10.800 --> 08:10.980
Right.

08:11.260 --> 08:19.800
But the more common tactic we see today is outsourcing the evaluation stage to parties who share the exact same financial incentives as the promoter.

08:19.800 --> 08:27.540
Oh, so like a VC firm that just invested $50 million in your AI startup is the one evaluating if your AI actually works.

08:28.660 --> 08:30.120
It's an incestuous loop.

08:30.360 --> 08:36.760
That brings up a really wild scenario that Wurzel mentioned at the end of his piece, which is this idea of retroactive vindication.

08:37.040 --> 08:38.800
The 2035 blood test scenario.

08:39.000 --> 08:39.280
Yes.

08:39.520 --> 08:41.980
Let's talk about this because this blew my mind.

08:41.980 --> 08:49.900
Wurzel quotes this investor who says, look, in 10 years, someone is going to invent a working, miniaturized blood test.

08:50.380 --> 08:54.720
And when they do, it's going to prove Elizabeth Holmes was just 20 years too early.

08:54.720 --> 08:57.480
It's the ultimate get out of jail free card for visionaries.

08:58.060 --> 08:59.580
Let me play devil's advocate here, though.

09:00.480 --> 09:08.480
If someone in 2035 actually invents a working Theranos box, doesn't that kind of vindicate Holmes's original vision?

09:08.960 --> 09:10.940
I mean, she saw the destination.

09:10.940 --> 09:14.080
Flyxion mathematically and logically dismantles this.

09:14.420 --> 09:16.900
It comes down to separating your decision from the outcome.

09:17.000 --> 09:17.160
Okay.

09:17.280 --> 09:17.940
How does that work?

09:18.380 --> 09:27.440
Ex-ante epistemic justification, meaning were you justified at the time, is based only on the evidence available to you at that exact moment.

09:27.740 --> 09:28.260
Time T.

09:28.480 --> 09:28.680
Right.

09:29.200 --> 09:33.160
Enlarging the information afterward doesn't change the premise of the original decision.

09:33.160 --> 09:43.920
If you lied in 2015 and said a machine was working in a lab and then someone completely different builds a working machine in 2035, your 2015 statement is still an objective lie.

09:44.540 --> 09:45.880
It's like playing poker, right?

09:46.300 --> 09:52.700
If I go all in pre-flop with a two and a seven offsuit, that is mathematically a terrible unjustified decision.

09:52.700 --> 09:54.500
A completely terrible decision.

09:54.840 --> 09:59.000
If the dealer then flops three sevens and I win the hand, I got lucky.

09:59.840 --> 10:03.700
But the positive outcome doesn't retroactively mean my strategy was smart.

10:04.120 --> 10:04.640
Exactly.

10:05.520 --> 10:07.540
Outcome and warrant are completely separate.

10:07.920 --> 10:10.640
You can be incredibly lucky without being justified.

10:10.640 --> 10:16.240
So, future success by other engineers can't retroactively repair your original lie.

10:16.400 --> 10:17.220
No, it cannot.

10:17.780 --> 10:27.220
But if they know they can't rely on retroactive vindication to save them legally or socially, how do these promoters protect themselves from being proven wrong in the present?

10:27.960 --> 10:28.900
They change the timeline.

10:29.600 --> 10:32.340
They introduce the concept of the unbounded horizon.

10:32.760 --> 10:34.000
The unbounded horizon.

10:34.560 --> 10:37.220
This is where Fleishen brings in Karl Popper, right?

10:37.620 --> 10:37.980
Yes.

10:38.320 --> 10:40.220
Popper's concept of falsifiability.

10:40.720 --> 10:46.040
For a scientific claim to be valid, there must be some conceivable observation that could prove it false.

10:46.260 --> 10:54.420
But if you make a claim over an unbounded future, like this technology will exist eventually, it's mathematically impossible to refute.

10:54.660 --> 10:55.220
Precisely.

10:55.220 --> 10:59.460
If year five rolls around and it fails, you just say it's arriving in year six.

10:59.920 --> 11:02.280
If it fails in year 50, you say year 51.

11:02.800 --> 11:06.080
No finite number of failures can ever prove the claim false.

11:06.080 --> 11:08.680
So it stops being science and just becomes dogma.

11:08.980 --> 11:09.100
Right.

11:09.680 --> 11:14.200
And Fleishen pairs this with Imre Lakatos' theory of research programs.

11:14.560 --> 11:15.460
Lakatos, right.

11:15.720 --> 11:16.280
Explain that one.

11:16.640 --> 11:21.580
Lakatos said a healthy scientific program makes risky predictions that can count against it.

11:22.080 --> 11:27.760
But a degenerating program, which is what we see in Silicon Valley, stops making testable predictions.

11:28.320 --> 11:29.280
What does it do instead?

11:29.280 --> 11:34.140
It creates an interpretive map, a protective belt around its core belief.

11:34.600 --> 11:37.660
Every single failure is absorbed and spun to protect the core.

11:37.960 --> 11:44.180
Here's where it gets really interesting because we see this interpretive map used in tech PR all the time.

11:44.300 --> 11:44.860
Oh, constantly.

11:44.860 --> 11:50.020
If a product launches and it's a total disaster, the narrative is never, our engineering failed.

11:50.880 --> 11:54.180
It's always, the consumer market just wasn't ready for our genius.

11:54.500 --> 11:54.720
Right.

11:54.880 --> 11:55.900
We were too early.

11:56.340 --> 12:02.040
Or if a project is delayed by six years, it's pitched as, the necessary cost of our incredible ambition.

12:02.880 --> 12:06.900
Every single outcome, no matter how bad, is read as a favorable indicator.

12:07.080 --> 12:09.380
It becomes completely unfalsifiable.

12:09.720 --> 12:11.560
But wait, I have to push back again.

12:11.560 --> 12:14.300
But isn't long-term thinking kind of essential?

12:15.020 --> 12:17.900
I mean, building power grids or space stations takes decades.

12:18.040 --> 12:18.360
Absolutely.

12:18.880 --> 12:22.520
Are we saying that no one is allowed to have a 50-year vision without being called a huckster?

12:23.260 --> 12:23.980
Not at all.

12:24.460 --> 12:27.960
And Flyxion clarifies this with a remedy called indexing.

12:28.360 --> 12:28.800
Indexing.

12:28.920 --> 12:29.120
Okay.

12:29.240 --> 12:29.840
How does that work?

12:30.540 --> 12:35.940
Long horizons are fine, but they have to be firmly tethered to stated interim indicators

12:35.940 --> 12:38.040
that actually have the capacity to fail.

12:38.360 --> 12:41.000
So you can't just say, we'll cure aging eventually.

12:41.560 --> 12:45.900
You have to say, we will hit this specific biomarker target by 2028.

12:46.440 --> 12:46.920
Exactly.

12:47.480 --> 12:51.140
And if 2028 arrives and you miss the target, the claim has formally failed.

12:51.760 --> 12:56.340
The pathology we are dealing with today is the unbounded horizon, combined with an absolute

12:56.340 --> 12:59.580
refusal to accept any outcome as a negative indicator.

12:59.960 --> 13:01.780
But why does the culture tolerate this?

13:02.140 --> 13:04.180
Like, venture capitalists are smart people.

13:04.600 --> 13:06.900
Why do they fund these unfalsifiable claims?

13:06.900 --> 13:09.280
To answer that, you have to follow the money.

13:09.660 --> 13:12.300
You have to look at the mathematical incentives of venture capital.

13:12.660 --> 13:14.920
Let's get into the math, because this blew my mind.

13:15.520 --> 13:20.080
The VC structure is built entirely on purchasing exposure to massive uncertainty.

13:20.500 --> 13:20.740
Right.

13:20.860 --> 13:22.300
The expected return formula.

13:22.900 --> 13:28.280
They know most startups will fail, but they are looking for that one massive 1,000x payoff

13:28.280 --> 13:30.000
that covers all the losses.

13:30.560 --> 13:31.000
Exactly.

13:31.000 --> 13:35.960
And the math isn't irrational for the VC, because their downside is capped at the cost

13:35.960 --> 13:36.980
of their initial investment.

13:37.660 --> 13:38.820
They only lose what they put in.

13:39.240 --> 13:43.620
But the real problem, and Flyxion stresses this, is the asymmetry of exposure.

13:44.020 --> 13:45.860
The asymmetry of exposure.

13:46.280 --> 13:46.580
Yes.

13:47.220 --> 13:51.940
An investor in a speculative medical device accepts the financial risk, but the patient in

13:51.940 --> 13:56.460
the hospital receiving a fake test result from that device did not price that risk into

13:56.460 --> 13:57.260
their life.

13:57.700 --> 13:57.960
Wow.

13:57.960 --> 14:03.980
The upside is completely privatized for the founders and investors, but the downside is

14:03.980 --> 14:06.520
transferred to workers, customers, and the public.

14:06.700 --> 14:08.480
The public absorbs the shock when it fails.

14:10.120 --> 14:12.760
Which brings us to the overstatement equation.

14:12.880 --> 14:14.840
This is proposition 3 in the paper.

14:15.560 --> 14:17.700
This equation is the key to understanding all of it.

14:17.960 --> 14:18.700
Let's walk through it.

14:18.700 --> 14:25.760
So it's US equals RS minus penalty minus lambda times external harm.

14:26.860 --> 14:28.920
Let's translate this math into real life.

14:30.040 --> 14:30.160
Okay.

14:30.320 --> 14:36.120
So US is the founder's expected utility or their net reward for hyping a product.

14:36.320 --> 14:36.420
Right.

14:36.420 --> 14:39.220
And R is the massive reward.

14:39.220 --> 14:41.500
The billions in funding the magazine covers.

14:42.440 --> 14:47.260
The penalty is the legal or social cost of getting caught lying, which in today's regulatory

14:47.260 --> 14:51.600
environment is shockingly close to zero if you use enough forward-looking statements.

14:51.600 --> 14:54.680
And then lambda is the coefficient of internalization.

14:54.900 --> 14:58.360
Basically, how much do you personally care about the harm you cause?

14:58.680 --> 14:59.120
Exactly.

14:59.380 --> 14:59.980
Do you feel guilty?

14:59.980 --> 15:06.040
So if I'm a founder, and the reward for hyping my product is billions of dollars, the penalty

15:06.040 --> 15:11.600
for puffery is zero, and I'm insulated enough that I don't feel the harm I'm causing society,

15:12.080 --> 15:14.080
why would I ever tell the truth?

15:14.380 --> 15:14.980
You wouldn't.

15:15.580 --> 15:19.020
It's like asking a soccer player not to flop when the ref is looking away.

15:19.520 --> 15:24.540
The penalty for diving is zero, and the reward is a game-winning penalty kick.

15:24.880 --> 15:26.560
That's a perfect analogy.

15:26.560 --> 15:31.640
If we connect this to the bigger picture, it perfectly explains Nikita Beer's quote in

15:31.640 --> 15:32.340
Warzel's piece.

15:32.600 --> 15:33.400
Remind me what he said.

15:33.600 --> 15:38.920
He basically said that faking it till you make it is actively excused by the adults in

15:38.920 --> 15:39.260
the room.

15:39.520 --> 15:41.320
Because it makes everyone money.

15:41.560 --> 15:41.800
Right.

15:42.180 --> 15:48.100
Accuracy is mathematically not a stable equilibrium when the marginal cost of overstating your

15:48.100 --> 15:50.000
capabilities is negligible.

15:50.320 --> 15:54.520
So epistemic distortion isn't just a moral lapse by one bad person.

15:54.520 --> 15:58.380
No, it's a stable evolutionary economic strategy.

15:58.920 --> 16:02.140
The market selects for the most aggressive exaggerator.

16:02.820 --> 16:07.400
Which brings us perfectly to the most pressing massive example of this entire framework, the

16:07.400 --> 16:07.960
AI boom.

16:08.420 --> 16:12.500
We have to tread carefully here because Warzel includes an important caveat that Flyxion

16:12.500 --> 16:13.120
builds on.

16:13.420 --> 16:15.180
Right, that AI actually is real.

16:15.400 --> 16:15.640
Yes.

16:16.160 --> 16:19.580
Chatbots, coding agents, multimodal models, they are functional.

16:20.140 --> 16:23.720
This isn't a crypto token or a metaverse with zero users.

16:23.720 --> 16:26.440
So the critique isn't that AI is totally fake.

16:26.800 --> 16:28.500
It's about how the claims are structured.

16:29.420 --> 16:29.820
Exactly.

16:30.900 --> 16:35.420
The AI field is aggressively blurring four completely different levels of claims under

16:35.420 --> 16:36.580
one umbrella term.

16:36.900 --> 16:38.680
Let's break down those four levels.

16:39.460 --> 16:40.980
Level one is observation.

16:41.620 --> 16:43.380
This is just a demonstrated fact.

16:43.880 --> 16:47.140
An AI bot successfully passes a Python coding benchmark.

16:47.820 --> 16:48.120
Okay.

16:48.760 --> 16:49.740
Empirical reality.

16:50.460 --> 16:52.140
Level two is extrapolation.

16:52.140 --> 16:56.880
This is assuming that because it passed the benchmark this year, the bot will improve

16:56.880 --> 17:01.060
at the exact same exponential rate next year without hitting any data walls.

17:01.760 --> 17:03.480
A bit of a leap, but okay.

17:04.280 --> 17:05.800
Level three is conjecture.

17:06.480 --> 17:12.160
This is assuming that simply scaling this current technology will magically result in an entirely

17:12.160 --> 17:16.840
undemonstrated, completely new architecture like artificial general intelligence.

17:16.840 --> 17:21.500
So going from a really good text predictor to an autonomous digital god.

17:21.920 --> 17:22.060
Right.

17:23.300 --> 17:25.780
And then level four is economic forecast.

17:26.160 --> 17:30.780
This is the sweeping claim that this AI will completely replace all human cognitive work and

17:30.780 --> 17:32.660
skyrocket global GDP.

17:32.660 --> 17:37.800
And Flyxion breaks down the specific economic forecast that Warzel cited, right?

17:38.240 --> 17:38.580
Yes.

17:38.620 --> 17:42.580
There was a model circulating that claimed U.S. economic output could rise from roughly

17:42.580 --> 17:47.280
$30 trillion to $44 trillion in just four years due to AI.

17:47.720 --> 17:50.180
Let's talk about the sheer math of that because that sounds insane.

17:50.700 --> 17:51.140
It is.

17:51.920 --> 17:52.980
Flyxion walks through the math.

17:52.980 --> 17:59.180
To jump from $30 trillion to $44 trillion in four years equates to roughly a 10% compound

17:59.180 --> 18:02.260
annual growth rate for the entire U.S. economy.

18:02.920 --> 18:06.040
Which is physically practically impossible for a mature economy.

18:06.440 --> 18:07.500
Completely impossible.

18:08.120 --> 18:13.260
The headline was generated by taking the most extreme, optimistic parameter setting of a theoretical

18:13.260 --> 18:17.660
economic model and quietly dropping all the defining constraints.

18:18.240 --> 18:20.080
So what does this all actually mean?

18:20.080 --> 18:24.700
If a coding bot completes a benchmark, promoters instantly leap all the way to level four and

18:24.700 --> 18:27.080
say, we will replace the entire U.S. economy.

18:27.880 --> 18:30.060
That's exactly the epistemic sleight of hand.

18:30.800 --> 18:35.180
It's like watching a toddler successfully stack three wooden blocks and immediately short-selling

18:35.180 --> 18:39.440
the entire commercial real estate market because this kid is going to build cheaper skyscrapers.

18:39.720 --> 18:41.380
That is precisely what is happening.

18:41.660 --> 18:43.440
And there's another rhetorical trick they use.

18:43.640 --> 18:47.760
They constantly compare a tech company's valuation to the global GDP.

18:48.240 --> 18:48.960
Oh, I've heard that.

18:48.960 --> 18:52.060
Like, this AI company is worth more than the GDP of France.

18:52.240 --> 18:52.500
Right.

18:53.140 --> 18:54.540
But it's mathematically illiterate.

18:54.700 --> 18:59.660
Valuation is a price for future cash flows stretching decades into an unbounded horizon.

19:00.540 --> 19:05.460
GDP is an annual flow of physical production in a single 365-day year.

19:05.600 --> 19:05.800
Okay.

19:05.880 --> 19:08.440
So you're comparing apples to, like, a concept of time.

19:08.720 --> 19:09.120
Exactly.

19:09.400 --> 19:14.540
You are comparing an optimistic projection of the future to a singular snapshot of reality.

19:14.540 --> 19:14.880
Okay.

19:15.360 --> 19:18.540
So the critique isn't that AI is a fraud like Theranos.

19:18.860 --> 19:23.360
The critique is that the industry is taking level one evidence, these basic observations,

19:23.800 --> 19:28.460
to demand authorization for massive level four societal commitments.

19:28.640 --> 19:35.140
They are demanding we restructure our energy grids and our labor laws based on a toddler stacking

19:35.140 --> 19:35.680
blocks.

19:35.680 --> 19:41.920
And this constant, frantic race toward the next massive level four breakthrough creates a terrifying

19:41.920 --> 19:44.920
blind spot regarding the things we've already built.

19:45.240 --> 19:51.300
Which brings us to Elizabeth Renieris' brilliant concept from the text, imaginative obsolescence.

19:52.460 --> 19:54.060
Imaginative obsolescence.

19:54.060 --> 19:55.820
What does that mean in practice?

19:56.540 --> 20:01.600
It's a state where one massive vision of the future replaces the next one so rapidly that

20:01.600 --> 20:05.400
our current vital systems are completely neglected and left to decay.

20:05.960 --> 20:08.080
This goes back to sustainability, the third pillar.

20:08.260 --> 20:08.600
Exactly.

20:09.640 --> 20:11.920
Civilization isn't just about constant innovation.

20:12.240 --> 20:14.100
It's largely about maintenance.

20:14.400 --> 20:14.540
Right.

20:15.020 --> 20:18.540
Inspecting bridges, patching software in hospitals, stewarding data.

20:18.900 --> 20:21.920
But maintenance doesn't generate 100x venture returns.

20:21.920 --> 20:25.280
A bridge not falling down isn't a liquidity event.

20:25.680 --> 20:27.300
Nobody wants to fund the guy holding the duct tape.

20:27.480 --> 20:27.680
Right.

20:28.340 --> 20:30.460
And Flyxion explains the math of this decay.

20:30.700 --> 20:34.940
There's a theorem in the text showing that a local component's viability can skyrocket

20:34.940 --> 20:38.060
while the global system's viability goes into the negative.

20:38.560 --> 20:39.580
Wait, walk me through that.

20:39.680 --> 20:42.600
How can a local system improve but the whole system fail?

20:42.940 --> 20:45.560
Think about a massive AI data center.

20:46.280 --> 20:50.500
The computational advance makes that specific AI model incredibly efficient.

20:50.500 --> 20:52.860
Its local viability goes way up.

20:53.200 --> 20:54.000
Okay, yeah, smarter.

20:54.440 --> 20:59.560
But that data center drains the regional energy grid, causing brownouts, spiking electricity

20:59.560 --> 21:02.820
costs for residents, and destabilizing the local labor market.

21:03.160 --> 21:04.860
The global system is weaker.

21:05.440 --> 21:05.780
Wow.

21:06.500 --> 21:11.680
This reminds me of a homeowner who installs, like, a state-of-the-art $50,000 smart home

21:11.680 --> 21:13.420
entertainment system in their living room.

21:13.420 --> 21:18.020
But they completely refuse to pay $500 to fix the leaky roof above it.

21:18.420 --> 21:19.240
That's exactly it.

21:19.640 --> 21:23.180
The local room is incredible, but the global house is literally rotting.

21:23.640 --> 21:28.020
Are we just building a society that only knows how to lay foundation but has no idea how to

21:28.020 --> 21:28.660
put on a roof?

21:29.220 --> 21:32.260
This raises an incredibly important question about authorization.

21:33.680 --> 21:38.400
How can we ever authorize the massive deployment of a technology based purely on a demonstration?

21:38.980 --> 21:42.400
Because you can't demonstrate maintenance in a one-hour keynote.

21:42.400 --> 21:43.220
You cannot.

21:44.100 --> 21:49.120
A system that externalizes the heavy cost of its own maintenance onto the public might

21:49.120 --> 21:54.360
look highly productive locally, but it's actually losing its ability to persist over time.

21:54.700 --> 22:00.480
So if our institutions are completely ignoring maintenance in reality, how do these companies

22:00.480 --> 22:05.320
react when they are finally confronted with hard, unavoidable evidence that their tech is failing?

22:05.860 --> 22:07.780
They engage in anti-repair.

22:08.240 --> 22:08.980
Anti-repair.

22:09.400 --> 22:11.920
This is the final diagnostic tool from the paper, right?

22:11.920 --> 22:12.400
Yes.

22:12.940 --> 22:19.540
It helps us determine if a company is operating on an empirical hypothesis or just a religious

22:19.540 --> 22:20.040
commitment.

22:20.520 --> 22:21.420
How do we know the difference?

22:22.280 --> 22:25.500
True correction means changing your model in response to new evidence.

22:25.740 --> 22:29.400
If the data says the machine doesn't work, you fix the machine or abandon the project.

22:29.780 --> 22:30.040
Okay.

22:30.280 --> 22:31.240
And anti-repair?

22:32.100 --> 22:36.460
Anti-repair is when you change your interpretation of the evidence in order to protect the model

22:36.460 --> 22:37.100
and the narrative.

22:37.440 --> 22:38.740
You move the goalposts.

22:38.740 --> 22:39.260
Exactly.

22:40.020 --> 22:45.080
If an organization cannot state exactly what specific evidence would cause them to officially

22:45.080 --> 22:47.100
abandon a project, it's not science anymore.

22:47.220 --> 22:48.540
It's a protected commitment.

22:48.540 --> 22:53.300
This brings up the psychological angle on Elizabeth Holmes that Wurtzel mentioned.

22:54.000 --> 22:58.360
Her entire identity was completely inseparable from being a builder.

22:58.940 --> 22:59.940
Identity enmeshment.

23:00.160 --> 23:00.360
Right.

23:00.720 --> 23:04.720
If admitting the blood machine doesn't work means admitting that you are a fundamental failure

23:04.720 --> 23:08.200
as a human being, you suddenly have two competing goals.

23:08.880 --> 23:12.220
Accuracy about the world and protecting your own soul.

23:12.220 --> 23:14.400
And self-preservation usually wins.

23:14.920 --> 23:16.440
You'll lie to the world to protect your ego.

23:16.760 --> 23:17.140
Exactly.

23:17.660 --> 23:21.360
And this leads Flyxion to a massive institutional lesson.

23:22.180 --> 23:27.580
Trustworthy institutions simply cannot rely on a founder's personal capacity for self-correction.

23:27.780 --> 23:28.620
Because they won't do it.

23:28.700 --> 23:28.880
Right.

23:29.280 --> 23:35.380
The procedures for evaluation, and more importantly for withdrawal, must exist entirely outside

23:35.380 --> 23:37.740
the person whose identity is on the line.

23:38.040 --> 23:40.340
We have to separate the evaluation from the ego.

23:40.780 --> 23:41.160
Absolutely.

23:41.160 --> 23:46.580
Well, this has been an incredibly heavy but completely necessary journey.

23:47.080 --> 23:47.980
Let's do a quick recap.

23:48.320 --> 23:49.320
We've covered a lot of ground.

23:49.740 --> 23:54.040
We learned how to separate possibility, the theoretical endpoint, from authorization,

23:54.640 --> 23:56.320
the demand for our money today.

23:56.740 --> 23:59.980
We've seen how unbounded horizons protect false profits.

24:00.400 --> 24:04.760
We saw how venture math mathematically incentivizes exaggeration over accuracy.

24:05.080 --> 24:11.040
We watched AI claims jump violently from level one observations to level four economic fantasies

24:11.040 --> 24:11.860
without evidence.

24:11.860 --> 24:18.660
And we explored why we are suffering from imaginative obsolescence, ignoring the present to chase

24:18.660 --> 24:19.200
the future.

24:19.200 --> 24:25.360
So, bringing it directly back to you, the listener, the next time you read a headline about a trillion

24:25.360 --> 24:28.440
dollar disruption, I want you to ask yourself a few questions.

24:28.780 --> 24:29.640
Apply the framework.

24:30.020 --> 24:30.440
Exactly.

24:31.140 --> 24:35.620
Ask, at what level is the actual evidence, and at what level is the promise?

24:35.620 --> 24:39.720
What are the interim indicators, and can they actually fail?

24:40.440 --> 24:44.800
Are you being sold a reachable path, or just an unfalsifiable possibility?

24:45.180 --> 24:49.580
I want to leave everyone with a final lingering idea that really builds on Flyxion's text.

24:49.660 --> 24:50.140
Go for it.

24:50.140 --> 24:54.860
If we continue to let founders treat the future as a blank check they can just cash today,

24:55.080 --> 24:57.480
we don't just risk some financial bubbles.

24:58.160 --> 25:02.880
What happens when the public fully realizes that none of these promises are indexed to reality?

25:03.020 --> 25:05.620
People would just stop believing in technology entirely.

25:05.800 --> 25:06.280
Exactly.

25:06.800 --> 25:12.320
We might face an era where genuine, necessary innovation, the kind we actually need to survive,

25:12.740 --> 25:13.840
is completely rejected.

25:13.840 --> 25:18.680
Because institutional trust will have been entirely burned to the ground by those who

25:18.680 --> 25:21.400
insisted they were never wrong, just early.

25:21.840 --> 25:25.300
That is a terrifying thought to end on, but a necessary one.

25:26.180 --> 25:28.420
Thank you all so much for joining us on this deep dive.

25:29.080 --> 25:33.300
Keep questioning the world around you, look past the stage lighting, and we will catch you

25:33.300 --> 25:33.680
next time.

